In the village where the river forgot to sing, there lived an old ferryman named Sila. Each dawn, he paddled a boat carved from driftwood, ferrying dreams across the water to a far shore no one could reach by feet alone. The villagers spoke softly of the tide that carried stories from one heart to another, but they spoke louder still of the coin that never rusted—the Coin of True Seeing—said to reveal a person’s true hunger.

One day, a young woman named Mira asked for passage with only a thread of courage tied around her wrist. She carried no coin, only a whisper of fear that clung like mist. Sila warned her that the ferry would reveal what she longed to hide. Mira nodded, stepping into the boat as the river swallowed the sun.

The boat drifted beneath a sky of glass. On the far shore, Mira found not gold or glory, but a mirror of her own hands, pale with caution. The river asked what she would do with the truth. Mira spoke of healing broken bridges, of feeding the hungry, of listening to the river’s song until every fear learned to swim. The boat returned empty, yet full of light. Sila smiled, knowing some journeys pay with courage, not coins. The village slept easier that night, listening for a river that finally remembered to sing.
